00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.430
So let's kind of just start
at a very high level why

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:07.040
people are looking at this
thing called multicloud.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.690
And if we kind of
step back in time

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:11.570
to the times of data
centers, I have never

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:14.150
stepped into a data center
where I saw a single vendor.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.550
So you didn't just have
Cisco or didn't just

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:20.240
have a single vendor for servers
or a single vendor for storage.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.950
You always had multiple
vendors in there.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.620
And that, I think, trend
continues even in the cloud.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:26.600
The only challenge
is in the cloud,

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.700
you have the owners,
or the cloud owners,

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.047
that own your data center.

00:02:30.047 --> 00:02:31.880
So in this case, you
have to actually double

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:32.680
your data centers.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.430
Or you have to have multiple
data centers instead

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:35.640
of the other way around.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.490
So the number one
reason we've heard

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.380
in terms of why
people go multicloud

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.410
is just this idea of
zero vendor lock-in.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:45.590
Even though cloud provides
you with a lot of resiliency

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:47.840
and availability, but if
you're using managed services

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.940
and if one of those
services goes down,

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:53.150
then you're kind of out of luck.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.800
Organizations want more control.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.610
And every cloud vendor does
cost a little bit differently.

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.470
Perhaps you have
credits with one cloud

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:01.670
and you want to use those.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:03.550
So that kind of
comes around a lot.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.050
Resiliency and availability--
I was working with a customer.

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:07.490
They were in a single region.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.260
They decided to go multi-region.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:10.910
And as I was talking
with them, they

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:14.660
said the benefit that they
got from going multicloud

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.160
and the work that
they had to do,

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.470
instead of going multi-region
with a single cloud

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:20.339
versus multi-region
with multiple clouds,

00:03:20.339 --> 00:03:21.380
was really not that much.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:23.540
And all those benefits
that they got,

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.540
they outweighed the work
that they had to do.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.720
Best of breed services
is usually another one.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.380
So we want to use
multiple clouds

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:33.380
because each cloud is doing
something better than others.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.890
At Google, we're known for
our data services, our ML,

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:40.520
and perhaps you're using some
proprietary services on prem,

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.770
so the idea that I want to
be able to use and choose

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.490
what I want to use.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.950
And then the last one comes
up maybe not as often,

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.430
and it's the idea of technology
and business partnerships.

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:52.970
You don't want to kind of hedge
your bets with a single vendor.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:56.150
And perhaps you want to
have multiple integrations

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.410
in multiple environments.

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:02.260
Really, it comes down
to, in one word, choice.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.650
Whether you're looking
as a business owner

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.150
or whether you're looking at
it from a technical standpoint.

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:09.830
A lot of the things that we
talked about here previously,

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:12.220
you can think of them
as business reasons

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:15.010
why people go multicloud
or multi-cluster

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:16.579
in that particular range.

00:04:16.579 --> 00:04:19.100
But when we look at it from
a developer's standpoint,

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.490
it really comes down
to freedom of choice.

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:24.310
So a developer's
productivity is directly

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.670
correlated to the
services that they can use

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:28.900
and they can choose.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:30.400
So if I'm a developer,
and if I want

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.860
to use BigQuery, or Spanner,
or any of the open source

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.840
products, or any of the
other managed services,

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:36.640
regardless of where
they're located,

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.274
I just need a safe
passage to get there.

00:04:39.274 --> 00:04:40.940
And I don't really
care how I get there,

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:43.570
I just want to be able
to describe what I want

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:45.300
and magically it
just appears to me.

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:49.630
Choice leads to chaos.

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:52.386
The more choice you
have, the more things

00:04:52.386 --> 00:04:53.510
are a little bit different.

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:54.730
They're heterogeneous.

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:58.660
And as operators, as DevOps,
we don't like heterogeneity.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:00.670
Because that leads
to something unsafe.

00:05:00.670 --> 00:05:05.980
It slows everything down, and
we want to be fast at iterating.

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:08.654
So the question comes, and
I get asked this a lot,

00:05:08.654 --> 00:05:10.320
should I be using
platform as a service?

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.060
And it's a great question.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.760
And perhaps you start with
a single platform and even

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.410
a platform as a
service, and then you

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:17.890
decide where you want to go.

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:20.015
The challenge in general
with platform as a service

00:05:20.015 --> 00:05:21.570
is they're very
opinionated systems.

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:24.810
So they kind of go
inversely proportional

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.640
to the idea of
developer's choice.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.860
So you may start
with a platform,

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.537
and you may kind of
lock your developers

00:05:30.537 --> 00:05:31.620
into what they want to do.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.078
And then eventually
your developers

00:05:33.078 --> 00:05:34.600
are going to want
something else.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:35.974
And then all of
a sudden, you are

00:05:35.974 --> 00:05:38.910
getting into this
heterogeneous world anyways.

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:42.245
So this is a cultural
type of a tipping point,

00:05:42.245 --> 00:05:43.620
that you have to
decide where you

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.110
are at that particular case.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:50.970
Let's switch focus just for a
minute to DevOps and operators.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:56.400
So in a very high level
way, our jobs as operators

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.230
is to take something
that's chaotic,

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.060
and take something
that's heterogeneous

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.400
and that's running in
these hybrid environments,

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.490
push it through some
magic box, and then

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:08.490
out comes something very safe,
and sound, and homogeneous,

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:09.750
because we like that.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:12.420
That's what we want to get to.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:17.100
And what goes in that circle
is what's in our opinion.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:19.819
We can make an
opinion about that.

00:06:19.819 --> 00:06:21.360
I want to take a
quick segue and talk

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.568
about these two words that
are in the middle, culture

00:06:23.568 --> 00:06:24.560
and tooling.

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.060
And I really want to start
with this idea of tooling.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:29.940
Whenever a new tool comes out,
like Kubernetes for example,

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.310
and all of a sudden it
gains a lot of hype,

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:34.050
everybody wants
to do Kubernetes.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:35.466
I don't even know
what that means.

00:06:35.466 --> 00:06:37.730
Like, when people say,
we need to do Kubernetes,

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.260
it just became a
verb or something.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.930
And then we force our
organizations, our people,

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.526
our culture to kind of
adhere to that tool.

00:06:45.526 --> 00:06:46.900
I think we're
doing it backwards.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:48.316
I think what we
need to do is kind

00:06:48.316 --> 00:06:51.720
of sit down with our business,
with our business goals,

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.480
and figure out exactly what
culture we want-- what culture

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.840
of development we want, what
culture of iteration we want,

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.790
what culture of
deployment we want,

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:03.270
and then pick the tools
that match that culture.

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:05.610
And I think that's also
the reason why tools also

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:08.465
get a lot of mixed reviews.

00:07:08.465 --> 00:07:09.840
Because what we're
doing is we're

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.207
trying to use a tool which
it wasn't meant to do.

00:07:12.207 --> 00:07:14.290
And we'll talk about that
here a little bit later.

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:17.040
So let's get back to
our regularly scheduled

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:19.040
programming.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:22.550
So talking about operators
and talking about DevOps here,

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:25.010
where developers
need more choice

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:27.770
and their productivity
really hinges on the services

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:31.820
that they can get to, it's our
job to get them there safely.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:33.754
The journey is
under our control.

00:07:33.754 --> 00:07:35.420
We can't really control
the destination,

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:37.070
but we can control the journey.

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:38.575
And I think that's
our true calling,

00:07:38.575 --> 00:07:39.950
is to make sure
that we give them

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:42.380
that golden path, that
safe passage, there.

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:45.470
And what goes in that middle
box of automation and control

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:47.360
and tooling, that's
something that we

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:48.590
can be opinionated about.

00:07:48.590 --> 00:07:52.000
And that's something
we can talk about.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.680
So a lot of people ask where
all these pieces fit in.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.830
So we have Kubernetes,
and Spinnaker, and Istio.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:57.663
What does that mean?

00:07:57.663 --> 00:07:59.200
And do I need all
of these things?

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:01.860
So this is kind of a simple
visual of where I think

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:03.570
everything kind of fits in.

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:06.360
So at the very bottom, you
have your business logic

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.112
that you convert into
code as a developer.

00:08:08.112 --> 00:08:09.570
And you need a way
to package that.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:10.990
And containerization
gave us that.

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:12.810
And we don't need to really
talk anything more about that.

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:14.768
I think we've been talking
about that to death.

00:08:14.768 --> 00:08:17.070
So containerization gives
us a great, efficient way

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:18.810
to package our code.

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:21.780
Then as you start deploying
containers in production

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:23.970
at scale, you need some
sort of an orchestrator,

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:25.620
some sort of a control
plane that manages that.

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:27.286
And that's where
orchestration comes in.

00:08:27.286 --> 00:08:30.017
It's a way to run
your applications.

00:08:30.017 --> 00:08:31.850
Now that you have your
applications running,

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:33.820
you need to manage the
lifecycle of those applications,

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:36.400
and that's where CI/CD tooling,
so continuous integration,

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:38.771
continuous deployment, and
delivery tools come in.

00:08:38.771 --> 00:08:40.270
And then this last
thing which we're

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:42.895
going to talk a little bit more
about later is this idea of now

00:08:42.895 --> 00:08:45.160
that we have hundreds, maybe
thousands of containers

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:47.770
running, how do we securely
connect and monitor

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:50.140
and manage the
connectivity of that?

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:52.930
And that's where the concept
of service mesh comes in.

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:56.504
And where some of
these tools fit in

00:08:56.504 --> 00:08:57.920
is something that
looks like this.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.380
We think Kubernetes is the best
place to run your containers.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:02.500
And we'll talk about why
that is and why it's really

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:04.550
gaining a lot of hype in
the developer community,

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:05.890
especially.

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:07.754
Spinnaker, again, is
one of those tools.

00:09:07.754 --> 00:09:09.170
It's a continuous
deployment tool.

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:09.940
We'll talk about that.

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:11.481
And especially,
we'll talk about that

00:09:11.481 --> 00:09:14.092
in the realm of multicloud
and hybrid cloud.

00:09:14.092 --> 00:09:15.550
And then some of
you may have heard

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:18.520
of Istio, which is an
implementation of service mesh

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:21.310
that allows us this kind of
a safe passage from container

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:22.840
to container.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:25.120
Now, two things I want to
say about this picture.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:28.600
One, first of all, I
think this forms a very

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:30.670
formidable platform for you.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:35.330
It's built of open source things
that you can deploy anywhere.

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:37.390
You don't have to have
every single component

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:38.680
that you see here.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:40.690
You start with
something kind of basic.

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:42.356
As a matter of fact,
a lot of the times,

00:09:42.356 --> 00:09:45.250
the journey starts with just
a platform, a Kubernetes

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:47.342
or an orchestrator
of your choice.

00:09:47.342 --> 00:09:49.550
And then you just make sure
that you can do that well

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:51.970
and you can do that better
before you kind of move

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:52.990
onto these tooling.

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:55.240
Another thing about this is
that a lot of these things

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:59.130
you can slide out and insert,
bring your own orchestrator

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:00.642
or bring your own CD tool.

00:10:00.642 --> 00:10:02.100
You're not stuck
with these things.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:03.850
We're just talking
about them as examples,

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:07.060
because these kind of fit
well in the multicloud space.

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:09.780
But you can slowly go
through this journey,

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:13.450
and you can exchange
these LEGO pieces to what

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:14.650
makes the most sense to you.

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:16.358
Because I do get asked
a lot of questions

00:10:16.358 --> 00:10:19.310
about Spinnaker versus Cloud
Build and stuff like that.

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:20.514
And again, it's a spectrum.

00:10:20.514 --> 00:10:22.930
It just depends upon what you
want out of your, let's say,

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:24.220
continuous deployment tool.

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:25.970
Or service meshes,
we've been doing them.

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:28.450
It was just called routing
and switching and firewalls.

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:29.800
And why do we need service mesh?

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:33.590
Again, it just depends upon
what you're trying to do.

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:35.590
So let's maybe start at
the bottom of the stack,

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:38.650
and kind of work our way
up, and then just see

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:40.430
how this all fits together.

00:10:40.430 --> 00:10:42.460
And then maybe we'll
even talk a little bit

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:44.680
about the journey of it.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:46.660
So Kubernetes, in
one sentence, it's

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:49.030
a way for developers to
describe their workloads.

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:51.490
And it just makes it happen
through a consistent API.

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:54.670
So it completely abstracts
you from cloud resources.

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:56.800
Your application
is a combination

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:58.210
of a bunch of resources.

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:00.550
There's VPCs and routing
and firewall rules

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:02.537
and VMs and load balancers.

00:11:02.537 --> 00:11:04.120
All of that combined,
your application

00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:05.661
is kind of hidden
in there somewhere.

00:11:05.661 --> 00:11:07.907
And Kubernetes gives
us that consistent API

00:11:07.907 --> 00:11:10.240
where developers can just
say, this is what I want done.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:11.980
This is my desired state.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:13.750
And what works well
with Kubernetes

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:16.390
is if you're in
heterogeneous environments

00:11:16.390 --> 00:11:19.030
or hybrid environments,
it will abstract you.

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:21.940
The way you do networking
on prem versus the way GCP

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:24.610
does networking, you don't
have to think about that

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:25.810
as a developer anymore.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:28.780
You can simply describe it
in a very consistent way,

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:32.370
and it will just make it happen.

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:35.550
As you start expanding, you may
start with a single cluster.

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:38.722
You'll probably end up
becoming multiple clusters,

00:11:38.722 --> 00:11:39.930
whether it's in single cloud.

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:41.430
So in this picture,
let's say you

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:43.470
start with a single
cluster, multi-tenant,

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:45.600
then eventually you go to
multiple clusters in GCP.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:49.440
Then you have some clusters
on prem, perhaps GKE On-Prem.

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:52.050
And then we have this one
cluster in some orange cloud

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:54.270
that no one's ever heard of.

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:58.030
So we start thinking about how
we're going to manage this.

00:11:58.030 --> 00:12:02.120
So first concept I want to talk
about is cluster consistency.

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:05.470
So the idea is kind of
similar to API consistency

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:06.580
with Kubernetes.

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:10.870
If we have multiple clusters,
if we configure them

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:14.230
in a consistent way, not only
do I get workload portability,

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:16.990
but I also get a consistent
way to describe my workload.

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:20.710
So if I'm a developer and I can
go around and I'm traveling,

00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:23.410
and let's say I just want to
get connected to my closest

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:26.440
cluster, I just want to be
able to describe my workload.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:27.910
And through the
magic of routing,

00:12:27.910 --> 00:12:30.520
I should be able to get
to where I want to go.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:32.020
And Kubernetes gives
us this concept

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:34.900
of namespaces, which are
just these logical containers

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:36.730
and abstraction
within a cluster that

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:40.480
divides your cluster
into different containers

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:43.300
that you can put authorization
and [INAUDIBLE] on top of that.

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:45.200
So an example here
is, for example,

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:47.410
if I'm a developer perhaps
I have my own namespace.

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:50.110
And I have some resource
quota within that namespace,

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:52.760
where inside of that namespace,
I can do whatever I want.

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:53.620
So I can develop.

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:55.210
I'm free to do whatever I want.

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:57.310
And then, perhaps, teams
have their own namespaces

00:12:57.310 --> 00:12:59.860
like my development team
or my engineering team.

00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:03.480
And they can collaborate
on top of that.

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:05.040
So a lot of people
ask the question,

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:08.190
when do I need multiple
clusters versus when

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:11.720
do I need namespace separation?

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:14.040
A common theme looks
something like this.

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:15.950
By the way, you don't
need 18 clusters.

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:18.740
This was just easier to cut,
copy, paste three times.

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:21.170
But essentially, an easy
way to think about this

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:24.740
is you will separate out
your phases of development.

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:28.970
So you'll have a separate
cluster or clusters for dev,

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:31.100
separate one for staging,
separate one for prod.

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:33.572
And that's a theme
that we've seen.

00:13:33.572 --> 00:13:35.030
And by the way,
this is by no means

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:37.220
an exhaustive list of
what you should do.

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:38.660
This is just some
common patterns

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:41.420
that have emerged as
we're talking to people.

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:44.030
Within an environment, so
within dev, within prod,

00:13:44.030 --> 00:13:46.432
within staging, they
look fairly consistent.

00:13:46.432 --> 00:13:48.890
And in this case, like I said,
I just cut, copy, pasted it.

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:51.732
However, you may have a
much smaller, let's say,

00:13:51.732 --> 00:13:53.690
dev environment, and a
completely different set

00:13:53.690 --> 00:13:56.660
of namespaces and permissions
than staging than prod.

00:13:56.660 --> 00:13:59.874
And your mileage may vary.

00:13:59.874 --> 00:14:01.540
So when you look at
a picture like this,

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:02.620
it looks quite daunting.

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:03.910
And then you start
thinking about, well,

00:14:03.910 --> 00:14:05.680
how am I going to manage
all of these things?

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:07.596
And how am I going to
manage applications that

00:14:07.596 --> 00:14:09.470
go into all of these things?

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:12.190
So again, going back
to the idea of that

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:15.190
when we find a tool that
we get a lot of force

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:17.020
behind, a lot of
community behind,

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:19.840
immediately we want that tool
to solve all the problems.

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:23.710
So immediately we say, oh, we
need some sort of federation.

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:25.197
That word comes up.

00:14:25.197 --> 00:14:27.280
Pretty much in any technology
that I've ever seen,

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:29.024
we need federation.

00:14:29.024 --> 00:14:30.940
And we don't even think
about what that means.

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:33.940
A lot of people just say, can
federation solve this problem?

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:37.120
Like, we need some networking
between this pod and this pod.

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:38.900
Can federation
solve this problem?

00:14:38.900 --> 00:14:40.150
Or we need cluster management.

00:14:40.150 --> 00:14:41.990
Can federation
solve this problem?

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:44.260
So we just immediately want
the tool, which is not,

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:47.020
by the way, designed to
do what it's meant to do.

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:49.040
We want that tool
to solve everything.

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:50.950
So Kubernetes was
really designed

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:53.470
to run containerized
applications

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:54.530
at scale in production.

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:56.174
And that was it.

00:14:56.174 --> 00:14:58.090
And then we started
talking about this concept

00:14:58.090 --> 00:14:59.680
of federation.

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:03.550
And I don't mean to
talk down on federation.

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:07.090
I'm just kind of delivering
the message of how we typically

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:09.527
think as a community
and how we immediately

00:15:09.527 --> 00:15:11.860
start to expand the scope of
what the product's actually

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:12.730
meant to do.

00:15:12.730 --> 00:15:15.530
So federation, for those
of us who may not know,

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:19.100
is an API that's abstracted
away from the cluster.

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:21.400
And it kind of gives you a
centralized control plane.

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:25.930
And the hope, or the dream,
is that that federation API

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:28.390
will magically just solve
all of your problems

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:30.070
from networking to
cluster management

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:33.430
to application management.

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:36.160
When you think of a picture
that looks something like this,

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:38.230
and you really look at
some of the use cases

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:40.814
and what are we trying to
do, and you split them up,

00:15:40.814 --> 00:15:43.480
it makes a little bit more sense
to see what tool makes the most

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:45.140
sense to solve the problem.

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:48.850
So first thing is the cluster
lifecycle management itself.

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:51.640
How do I manage the policy and
the lifecycle of the cluster?

00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:52.900
That's one problem.

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:55.000
Then you have application
lifecycle management.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:56.916
How do I manage the
applications that actually

00:15:56.916 --> 00:15:57.880
get put on top of that?

00:15:57.880 --> 00:15:58.900
That's another one.

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:03.220
And then you have kind of
the service, discovery,

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:06.070
cross-cluster, integration,
and that problem.

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:08.846
And we want federation to
solve all of these problems.

00:16:08.846 --> 00:16:10.220
And it may be the
right solution.

00:16:10.220 --> 00:16:10.660
We don't know.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:12.034
It may not be the
right solution.

00:16:12.034 --> 00:16:13.460
It may be the right solution.

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:15.080
What we're starting
to see, there

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:17.020
were a couple of logistical
problems with the federation

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:17.519
API.

00:16:17.519 --> 00:16:19.240
There were only a
handful of people

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:21.580
that were working on the
federation API versus thousands

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:24.450
and thousands of people that
were working on the Kubernetes

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:24.950
API.

00:16:24.950 --> 00:16:27.820
And it was just almost nearly
impossible to keep that feature

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:28.379
parity.

00:16:28.379 --> 00:16:30.670
And on top of that, when you
have a centralized control

00:16:30.670 --> 00:16:32.020
plane and something
goes wrong, it just

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:33.415
cascades across everything.

00:16:33.415 --> 00:16:37.160
So when you make one mistake,
it just goes through that.

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:39.070
So whether you call it
cluster independence--

00:16:39.070 --> 00:16:41.046
I don't know if that's
a really good name.

00:16:41.046 --> 00:16:42.920
I know the community
called it multi-cluster.

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:45.340
Just the idea here is that
if you treat each cluster

00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:48.070
kind of isolated islands,
and you just overlaid them

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:51.730
with components and tooling
that gives you cluster lifecycle

00:16:51.730 --> 00:16:54.130
management, that gives
you application lifecycle

00:16:54.130 --> 00:16:56.290
management, it might be a
better way to do things,

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:59.140
because you can isolate
the blast radius when

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:00.936
something bad happens.

00:17:00.936 --> 00:17:02.560
So from a cluster
lifecycle management,

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:03.830
we're not going to talk
too much about that.

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:05.720
I know there's some
announcements coming

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:08.170
with the GKE On-Prem and
multi-cluster management.

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:10.170
Hopefully you saw
some of the demos.

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:12.250
I'm really going to go
into application lifecycle

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:15.099
management and then service,
cross-cluster service

00:17:15.099 --> 00:17:18.579
discovery and kind of
traffic management piece.

00:17:18.579 --> 00:17:21.540
So let's look at an application
running on Kubernetes.

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:23.310
So again, we have
multiple clusters.

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:25.920
And an application on
Kubernetes is also a combination

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:27.079
of a bunch of resources.

00:17:27.079 --> 00:17:29.490
So you have deployments,
and deployments

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:31.440
are running replicas of pods.

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:33.620
So in this case, we
have an application.

00:17:33.620 --> 00:17:36.000
Let's say this is a web
front end application running

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:37.834
and two versions of
that deployment running.

00:17:37.834 --> 00:17:39.583
We have a red version
and a black version,

00:17:39.583 --> 00:17:40.620
three pods running each.

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:42.540
They're upended by
a service, and then

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:43.956
let's say this is
a web front end.

00:17:43.956 --> 00:17:46.434
So you're exposing it
out through an ingress.

00:17:46.434 --> 00:17:47.850
And since this is
a web front end,

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:49.870
you want that application
running everywhere.

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:51.600
So the picture looks
something like this.

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:53.266
You have a bunch of
these resources that

00:17:53.266 --> 00:17:55.966
are kind of distributed across
clusters, across namespaces,

00:17:55.966 --> 00:17:57.840
and then if you put a
box around all of this,

00:17:57.840 --> 00:17:59.920
that's what your application
actually looks like.

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:01.840
And from a developer's
perspective,

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:03.690
I don't want to go
into each cluster

00:18:03.690 --> 00:18:05.830
and start looking at what
my deployment looks like,

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:08.140
what my ingress looks like,
what my service looks like.

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:11.880
So platforms and even
cloud resources in general

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:13.530
are a very
resource-centric-- give you

00:18:13.530 --> 00:18:15.870
a very resource-centric
view of your application.

00:18:15.870 --> 00:18:18.416
They don't really tell you
what your application is doing.

00:18:18.416 --> 00:18:19.290
So hold that thought.

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:20.850
When we go to Spinnaker,
we'll talk a little bit more

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:21.424
about that.

00:18:21.424 --> 00:18:23.715
But I really want to talk
about where Spinnaker fits in

00:18:23.715 --> 00:18:25.560
in this application
lifecycle management

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:27.640
and how we see CI/CD at Google.

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:30.000
So from converting business
logic into source code,

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.990
we have source repository,
to building and unit testing

00:18:32.990 --> 00:18:36.600
your artifacts, and that's
where Cloud Build comes in.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:39.540
Hopefully you've heard some of
the announcements around there.

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:42.720
To artifact storage
through Container Registry,

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:44.880
and that's our own private
container repository,

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:46.830
very similar to Docker
Hub that's backed up

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:48.090
by Cloud Storage.

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:49.620
And where Spinnaker
really fits in

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:52.590
is in the continuous delivery
and continuous deployment

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:53.130
thing.

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:56.220
So that's what Spinnaker
is meant to do.

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:57.660
And again, this is a spectrum.

00:18:57.660 --> 00:18:59.760
There are a myriad
of tools that you

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:01.740
can use to manage your
application lifecycle.

00:19:01.740 --> 00:19:04.170
And it depends upon
where in your journey

00:19:04.170 --> 00:19:06.240
are you in that
particular stage.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:08.740
So you can use Cloud
Build to do everything

00:19:08.740 --> 00:19:11.140
if you don't need, let's say,
automated canary analysis,

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:12.515
which we'll talk
about, or if you

00:19:12.515 --> 00:19:15.990
don't need our back integration
with your LDAP and stuff

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:18.290
like that.

00:19:18.290 --> 00:19:19.734
So what is Spinnaker?

00:19:19.734 --> 00:19:21.150
For those of you
who may not know,

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:22.710
Spinnaker is an
open source tool.

00:19:22.710 --> 00:19:23.960
It was open source by Netflix.

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:25.668
It's a continuous
delivery and deployment

00:19:25.668 --> 00:19:29.300
tool that works really well
across hybrid and multicloud

00:19:29.300 --> 00:19:30.120
environments.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:32.670
And by hybrid I mean both
in different clouds--

00:19:32.670 --> 00:19:34.759
let's say on prem and
GCP or something--

00:19:34.759 --> 00:19:36.300
as well as in
different environments.

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:40.560
So you could do
VMs and Kubernetes.

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:43.480
It gives you a very
application-centric view

00:19:43.480 --> 00:19:44.410
of your application.

00:19:44.410 --> 00:19:45.740
So in this particular example--

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:47.957
this is a screen shot,
one of the screen shots,

00:19:47.957 --> 00:19:50.290
of Spinnaker-- we have an
application called MD Service.

00:19:50.290 --> 00:19:52.270
And you can see that
right at the top left.

00:19:52.270 --> 00:19:54.160
And then I can
see exactly what's

00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:56.231
happening with that
particular service.

00:19:56.231 --> 00:19:58.480
So that particular service
is running in two clusters.

00:19:58.480 --> 00:19:59.899
Those are the red boxes.

00:19:59.899 --> 00:20:01.815
I can see which namespaces
they're running on.

00:20:01.815 --> 00:20:03.648
So they're running in
the default namespace.

00:20:03.648 --> 00:20:05.750
I can see how many
deployments are running.

00:20:05.750 --> 00:20:09.880
So in the prod version,
which is the top cluster,

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:12.660
I have one deployment running,
which is kind of the big box.

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:15.130
And then I can also see
the green rectangles

00:20:15.130 --> 00:20:17.195
are the individual
pods that are running.

00:20:17.195 --> 00:20:18.820
And I can see them
scaling up and down.

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:19.810
And since they're
green, that means

00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:21.460
everything is nice and healthy.

00:20:21.460 --> 00:20:22.630
Everything is good to go.

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:24.460
And in my staging
cluster, I can see

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:25.690
that I have two pods running.

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:28.870
And I also can keep
track of my existing

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:30.580
iterations of that deployment.

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:32.649
Also, if I click on
any of these resources,

00:20:32.649 --> 00:20:34.940
I can get a much more detailed
view of what's going on.

00:20:34.940 --> 00:20:36.370
So if I click on
the deployment, I

00:20:36.370 --> 00:20:37.953
can see how many
replicas I'm running.

00:20:37.953 --> 00:20:42.262
I can even see the
container logs and so on.

00:20:42.262 --> 00:20:43.720
Everything in
Spinnaker is deployed

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:45.550
in something called
a pipeline, which

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:48.160
may be not a foreign concept
to a lot of you, which

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:50.500
is, if I want to get from
version 1 to version 2,

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:52.810
I need to go through a
series of steps and actions

00:20:52.810 --> 00:20:53.860
that are interdependent.

00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:56.990
And they really depend upon
what's happening before.

00:20:56.990 --> 00:20:57.992
So this is an example.

00:20:57.992 --> 00:20:59.950
And those steps, by the
way, are called stages.

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:03.100
So this is an example of a
fairly common pipeline, where

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:04.810
I've taken something
out of staging

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:07.420
and I want to now
eventually get to production

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:08.890
that particular piece of code.

00:21:08.890 --> 00:21:10.177
So I grab that image.

00:21:10.177 --> 00:21:12.135
I deploy it as something
called a canary, which

00:21:12.135 --> 00:21:13.260
we'll talk about.

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:15.100
I wait a few
minutes to make sure

00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:16.960
that that thing is looking good.

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:20.870
If everything passes, then I
promote that to production.

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:23.140
I tear down the old
production, and I tear down

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:24.389
the canary that I've deployed.

00:21:24.389 --> 00:21:29.990
And I promote that particular
image to production.

00:21:29.990 --> 00:21:33.650
Even though Kubernetes comes
with some elementary way

00:21:33.650 --> 00:21:36.830
to manage applications or manage
services like rolling updates

00:21:36.830 --> 00:21:39.620
and stuff, perhaps you need
a little bit more horsepower.

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:42.920
And Spinnaker gets
you that extra mile.

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:44.600
So there are
deployment strategies

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:46.580
built in, like red-black,
rolling red-black,

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:49.156
or canary are kind of the
three more popular ones.

00:21:49.156 --> 00:21:51.530
So red-black, or sometimes
called blue-green, is the idea

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:53.613
that I have one version
of an application running.

00:21:53.613 --> 00:21:56.270
So in this case, we have the
blue version with eight pods

00:21:56.270 --> 00:21:57.320
running, let's say.

00:21:57.320 --> 00:22:00.830
And then when I want to deploy
a new version, version green,

00:22:00.830 --> 00:22:02.250
I also will deploy
in eight pods.

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:04.730
And then I will just flip
the load balancer over.

00:22:04.730 --> 00:22:06.980
Great thing is I can very
quickly and immediately

00:22:06.980 --> 00:22:10.285
move back to the blue version
if something goes wrong.

00:22:10.285 --> 00:22:12.410
And the bad thing is I need
twice as many resources

00:22:12.410 --> 00:22:16.580
because I have both versions
running for a period of time.

00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:19.070
Kind of the new iteration of
that is the rolling red-black

00:22:19.070 --> 00:22:21.530
or rolling
blue-green, which is I

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:24.050
will gradually roll
down one instance

00:22:24.050 --> 00:22:25.590
as I bring up the
other instance.

00:22:25.590 --> 00:22:27.920
And every so often,
I will just make sure

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:29.960
that everything is
going very smoothly.

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:32.390
And I'll have these
validation gates.

00:22:32.390 --> 00:22:35.600
And canary is another
iteration of rolling red-black.

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:37.100
Really, the idea
here is that I will

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:38.720
deploy a canary, a
very small version,

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:41.420
and I will direct production
traffic to that canary.

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:44.240
And then I will test
that canary for a number

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:47.090
of metrics against a baseline
of production traffic.

00:22:47.090 --> 00:22:48.590
And then based on
all these metrics,

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:51.030
I will give it a
score, a canary score.

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:54.030
And then if that score is
passable or high enough,

00:22:54.030 --> 00:22:56.330
then I will promote
that to production.

00:22:56.330 --> 00:22:57.800
And really what
makes it different

00:22:57.800 --> 00:22:59.600
is that canary analysis
piece and the idea

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:02.050
that I'm actually sending
production traffic,

00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:06.042
and I'm actually comparing
it to production baselines.

00:23:06.042 --> 00:23:07.500
Some other things
that are built in

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:09.958
are just the idea that I can
dictate which days and times I

00:23:09.958 --> 00:23:11.910
want these pipelines to run.

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:13.990
I can have manual intervention.

00:23:13.990 --> 00:23:17.700
So I can say, go to
step C and then pause.

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:21.610
And then let's wait for someone
to actually push the Continue

00:23:21.610 --> 00:23:22.110
button.

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:23.730
And I can roll
back quite easily,

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:26.800
because Spinnaker keeps track
of historical views of what's

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:27.550
happening as well.

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:31.690
Lots and lots of
integrations-- these

00:23:31.690 --> 00:23:33.581
are just a bunch of logos.

00:23:33.581 --> 00:23:34.580
Triggers are a good one.

00:23:34.580 --> 00:23:36.710
So you can trigger pipelines.

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:38.590
So you can have all
these templated pipelines

00:23:38.590 --> 00:23:40.750
already built for each
application, maybe even

00:23:40.750 --> 00:23:42.234
multiple pipelines.

00:23:42.234 --> 00:23:43.900
And you just need a
way to trigger them.

00:23:43.900 --> 00:23:47.860
So an easy way is anytime a
code changes or a version tag

00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:51.490
changes-- and I'll show you
an example of that shortly--

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:52.640
just run the pipeline.

00:23:52.640 --> 00:23:55.450
So think of that pipeline
as kind of that paved path

00:23:55.450 --> 00:23:57.160
to the next version.

00:24:00.930 --> 00:24:05.920
Let's talk a little bit about
Istio and service meshes.

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:08.470
So we have Kubernetes deployed.

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:12.280
We feel very good about just
our Kubernetes environment.

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:15.490
Then we started deploying some
sort of a CD tool or CI/CD,

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:16.420
maybe Spinnaker.

00:24:16.420 --> 00:24:17.170
And we feel great.

00:24:17.170 --> 00:24:20.080
Now we have multiple versions
of our applications and services

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:22.310
running in multiple
environments.

00:24:22.310 --> 00:24:24.190
And all these need to
now talk to each other,

00:24:24.190 --> 00:24:26.170
and we don't know
exactly how to do this.

00:24:26.170 --> 00:24:27.380
So somebody smart said this.

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:29.920
I still don't know who said
it, but I think very, very wise

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:31.570
words by somebody.

00:24:31.570 --> 00:24:33.940
I have no idea how everything
is talking to each other.

00:24:33.940 --> 00:24:38.039
And more importantly, I can't
observe exactly what's going on

00:24:38.039 --> 00:24:39.080
in this particular thing.

00:24:39.080 --> 00:24:40.750
And that's kind of
where Istio comes in.

00:24:40.750 --> 00:24:43.900
And before we talk about Istio,
let's spend a few minutes

00:24:43.900 --> 00:24:45.775
on service meshes, and
what is a service mesh

00:24:45.775 --> 00:24:48.190
and why do we need it?

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:50.080
There was a pretty
big, long sentence,

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:51.790
and then I was just trying to
keep it shorter and shorter.

00:24:51.790 --> 00:24:53.680
And this was kind
of the shortest form

00:24:53.680 --> 00:24:56.050
of that sentence, which is
transparently automating

00:24:56.050 --> 00:24:57.700
network application functions.

00:24:57.700 --> 00:25:00.700
Or even more simply, I want
to basically take the business

00:25:00.700 --> 00:25:03.710
logic away from any of the
networking functionality.

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:06.550
So anything to do with
networking, security,

00:25:06.550 --> 00:25:08.560
observability, I want
to abstract that away

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:11.380
from my core code, my
core business code.

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:14.290
That's really what
a service mesh is.

00:25:14.290 --> 00:25:16.020
Lots and lots words
on this slide--

00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:17.270
this is not my favorite slide.

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:18.680
You don't have to read it.

00:25:18.680 --> 00:25:20.980
But just the four
categories that we kind of

00:25:20.980 --> 00:25:21.820
want to mention.

00:25:21.820 --> 00:25:24.430
Now that we have multiple
versions of multiple services

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:26.440
running, we want to be
able to control which

00:25:26.440 --> 00:25:29.140
version of service A can
talk to which version

00:25:29.140 --> 00:25:32.410
of service B, which user
can talk to which front end,

00:25:32.410 --> 00:25:32.910
and so on.

00:25:32.910 --> 00:25:34.840
So we need some intelligent
and more advanced

00:25:34.840 --> 00:25:38.260
routing than what Kubernetes
natively gives us.

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:39.220
Resiliency.

00:25:39.220 --> 00:25:39.884
Timeouts.

00:25:39.884 --> 00:25:41.175
Circuit breakers is a huge one.

00:25:41.175 --> 00:25:42.260
If I have cascading
failures, I want

00:25:42.260 --> 00:25:44.719
to be able to open that
circuit for x amount of time,

00:25:44.719 --> 00:25:46.510
instead of just that
service going haywire.

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:48.130
Health checks.

00:25:48.130 --> 00:25:50.680
Security and policy
is even bigger.

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:53.800
So every service needs to
have some verifiable form

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:55.490
of identifying itself
to other services.

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:57.230
When two services
talk to each other,

00:25:57.230 --> 00:25:59.830
I want to be able to tell
with great confidence

00:25:59.830 --> 00:26:02.839
that I am who I
am, who I say I am.

00:26:02.839 --> 00:26:04.630
And I want to be able
to talk to each other

00:26:04.630 --> 00:26:05.950
in an encrypted fashion.

00:26:05.950 --> 00:26:07.900
And then policy
enforcement-- perhaps I

00:26:07.900 --> 00:26:12.780
want to put limits
on RPS and QPS

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:16.710
and how much rate can one
service talk to another.

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:18.870
Perhaps you have a free
version or freemium

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:19.770
version of a service.

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:21.150
And you can say, I'm
going to rate limit that.

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:22.525
And then you have
a paid version,

00:26:22.525 --> 00:26:23.839
and you can open that up.

00:26:23.839 --> 00:26:25.630
And then last probably
should be the first,

00:26:25.630 --> 00:26:27.480
which is the idea of I also
want to observe and see

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:28.630
exactly what's going on.

00:26:28.630 --> 00:26:30.338
So I want to see the
logs and the metrics

00:26:30.338 --> 00:26:32.397
and especially distribute
a tracing, which really

00:26:32.397 --> 00:26:34.230
kind of comes into being
when you're talking

00:26:34.230 --> 00:26:36.940
about multiple clusters.

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:40.500
So a quick history
of load balancers.

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:42.624
Matt Klein wrote a
fantastic blog on Medium.

00:26:42.624 --> 00:26:44.540
If you haven't seen it,
I highly recommend it.

00:26:44.540 --> 00:26:45.930
It's a little bit
of a long read,

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:47.889
and I'm sure we'll share
these slides with you.

00:26:47.889 --> 00:26:50.388
Or you can just type "Matt Klein
history of load balancing."

00:26:50.388 --> 00:26:52.770
I'm going to give you the
three-minute version of what

00:26:52.770 --> 00:26:55.780
he talks about on kind of where
we were and where we are now.

00:26:55.780 --> 00:26:57.550
So the idea, again,
is very simple.

00:26:57.550 --> 00:27:01.020
If you look at the top two
versions of load balancing,

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:02.562
they're fairly similar.

00:27:02.562 --> 00:27:04.020
I have a bunch of
clients that want

00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:06.810
to talk to a bunch of services,
a bunch of back end services.

00:27:06.810 --> 00:27:11.330
So I go through a single
gateway, a centralized gateway,

00:27:11.330 --> 00:27:13.550
or a proxy, like an F5,
let's say, in a data

00:27:13.550 --> 00:27:14.980
center or something like that.

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:17.004
And then it fans out
my requests back,

00:27:17.004 --> 00:27:18.420
which means
everything has to talk

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:19.930
to that centralized gateway.

00:27:19.930 --> 00:27:22.305
So that second one is kind of
an iteration of that, maybe

00:27:22.305 --> 00:27:25.110
internal load balancer
versus external.

00:27:25.110 --> 00:27:26.436
The challenge with this--

00:27:26.436 --> 00:27:28.560
and that's what we call
server-side load balancing.

00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:31.865
The challenge with this is in
a micro services environment

00:27:31.865 --> 00:27:33.990
where every service needs
to talk to every service,

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:35.880
you're paying that extra hop.

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:38.460
So if everything is going
to a centralized gateway,

00:27:38.460 --> 00:27:40.560
that model may not be
the most efficient.

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:43.230
It may work, again,
for a period of time.

00:27:43.230 --> 00:27:45.180
And then eventually,
you'll grow beyond that.

00:27:45.180 --> 00:27:46.890
And then you're
paying that extra hop.

00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:49.350
So that's where the concept of
client libraries came about,

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:52.620
which is, again, I'm
going to take anything

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:54.240
that I need for
networking, and I'm

00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:55.950
going to shove that
into a library.

00:27:55.950 --> 00:27:58.080
And then I'm going to
force my developers

00:27:58.080 --> 00:28:01.911
to use that library with every
service that they create.

00:28:01.911 --> 00:28:02.660
So it works great.

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:05.030
Day one, you have one language,
and you have one version

00:28:05.030 --> 00:28:06.080
of the client library.

00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:07.550
And you force your developers.

00:28:07.550 --> 00:28:08.984
They do a very good job.

00:28:08.984 --> 00:28:11.150
And then all of a sudden,
you have multiple versions

00:28:11.150 --> 00:28:12.920
of that library in one language.

00:28:12.920 --> 00:28:14.450
And then all of a sudden,
you have multiple languages

00:28:14.450 --> 00:28:16.033
and multiple versions
of that library.

00:28:16.033 --> 00:28:19.070
And oh, by the way, developers
are not networking experts,

00:28:19.070 --> 00:28:20.240
are not security experts.

00:28:20.240 --> 00:28:22.370
So any of the vulnerabilities
that you have,

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:25.010
they are just going to blindly
put that into their services.

00:28:25.010 --> 00:28:26.810
And you have this
very tight coupling

00:28:26.810 --> 00:28:30.730
between services and that
networking functionality.

00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:32.440
So that kind of gave
birth to this idea

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:35.470
that I want to take all of
this networking and security

00:28:35.470 --> 00:28:38.350
and observability, and
I want to run that out

00:28:38.350 --> 00:28:42.680
of process, out of my core
service process, as a side car,

00:28:42.680 --> 00:28:44.830
or think of it as a valet.

00:28:44.830 --> 00:28:46.840
So instead of you
talking directly to me,

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:49.120
you will talk to a valet
and then say, go ahead

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:51.010
and deliver my message.

00:28:51.010 --> 00:28:53.524
So now we have an interesting
picture, just at the bottom.

00:28:53.524 --> 00:28:55.690
By the way, this is what's
called a client-side load

00:28:55.690 --> 00:28:58.960
balancing, where I've taken
all of the load balancing

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:00.730
and routing and
switching functionality,

00:29:00.730 --> 00:29:04.010
and I've moved it
out of process.

00:29:04.010 --> 00:29:06.610
Now we can draw a line in
between those two services,

00:29:06.610 --> 00:29:08.380
and we can have
clear delineation

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:10.150
of roles and responsibility.

00:29:10.150 --> 00:29:12.450
Above that line is
my business logic.

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:13.700
My developers can handle that.

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:14.866
That's what they're good at.

00:29:14.866 --> 00:29:15.700
Let them do that.

00:29:15.700 --> 00:29:18.200
Below that line, our
operators, our network admins,

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:19.610
our DevOps can handle that.

00:29:19.610 --> 00:29:21.130
That's what they're good at.

00:29:21.130 --> 00:29:27.840
Also, the services at
this point have no idea

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:29.400
of their networking
topology, which

00:29:29.400 --> 00:29:32.280
works great in this
multicloud, hybrid cloud,

00:29:32.280 --> 00:29:35.080
heterogeneous environments
that we're talking about.

00:29:35.080 --> 00:29:37.950
So a way to look at it is,
you have a bunch of services,

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:40.500
and they each have
a side car running.

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:44.225
And visually if you think
about two concentric circles.

00:29:44.225 --> 00:29:46.350
You have an outside circle
with all these services,

00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:49.430
and then you have an inside
circle with all these proxies.

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:51.930
Outside circle has no
concept of the inside circle

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:53.460
or any of the topology.

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:57.060
You can even just pick that
up and put another one in.

00:29:57.060 --> 00:29:59.820
And these proxies are the ones
that are talking to each other.

00:29:59.820 --> 00:30:02.278
And the developers are just
kind of sitting on the outside,

00:30:02.278 --> 00:30:05.092
just developing and just putting
business logic into code.

00:30:05.092 --> 00:30:06.550
So that's one way
of looking at it.

00:30:06.550 --> 00:30:07.980
Another interesting
thing about this picture

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:09.180
is it looks very clean.

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:12.370
You have this box
around these two things.

00:30:12.370 --> 00:30:14.820
So in this world of
containerization,

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:16.230
these two could
be containerized.

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:18.270
And it should be put
in some abstraction.

00:30:18.270 --> 00:30:19.560
And in Kubernetes,
that works really well,

00:30:19.560 --> 00:30:21.330
because we have something
called pods that

00:30:21.330 --> 00:30:22.649
can run one or more containers.

00:30:22.649 --> 00:30:24.690
So in this particular
case, we have one container

00:30:24.690 --> 00:30:26.640
running our service
and then one container

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:28.860
running our proxy
or our side car.

00:30:28.860 --> 00:30:30.554
And that's where that
concept comes in.

00:30:30.554 --> 00:30:31.470
So what is that proxy?

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:34.610
Anybody heard of Envoy?

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:36.450
Half the people, great.

00:30:36.450 --> 00:30:37.680
Envoy is a proxy.

00:30:37.680 --> 00:30:40.110
At its heart, at its core,
it's just a layer 4 proxy, just

00:30:40.110 --> 00:30:41.580
like Nginx, HAProxy.

00:30:41.580 --> 00:30:43.770
It has layer 7 filters built in.

00:30:43.770 --> 00:30:45.730
It can speak a lot of
different protocols.

00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:47.910
It's HTTP/2 first.

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:49.410
It's very scalable,
very resilient.

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:50.540
It's very lightweight.

00:30:50.540 --> 00:30:54.000
So the lightweight part makes
it really interesting and really

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:55.454
efficient to work in that.

00:30:55.454 --> 00:30:57.120
By the way, you don't
have to use Envoy.

00:30:57.120 --> 00:31:00.060
But I just mention that, because
it does a few things pretty

00:31:00.060 --> 00:31:00.770
interesting.

00:31:00.770 --> 00:31:03.360
So the way you can
figure Envoy at runtime

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:05.460
is through something
called Discovery Services.

00:31:05.460 --> 00:31:07.918
And together, they're called
Aggregated Discovery Services,

00:31:07.918 --> 00:31:09.000
or ADS.

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:10.582
So Listener Discovery
Service-- what

00:31:10.582 --> 00:31:12.540
ports should I be listening
to and how should I

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:13.415
be listening to them?

00:31:13.415 --> 00:31:16.080
Route Discovery Services--
how should I route traffic

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:17.820
as it comes in and goes?

00:31:17.820 --> 00:31:19.410
Cluster and endpoints,
you can almost

00:31:19.410 --> 00:31:22.560
think of them as services and
pods in the Kubernetes lingo.

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:24.300
So a cluster is a
service in Kubernetes,

00:31:24.300 --> 00:31:27.090
and endpoints are pods or
endpoints within communities.

00:31:27.090 --> 00:31:29.280
And they can discover
that at runtime live.

00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:30.930
They don't have to be restarted.

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:34.120
The proxy doesn't
have to be restarted.

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:35.590
Which now brings us to Istio.

00:31:35.590 --> 00:31:39.720
Istio is just an open
source implementation

00:31:39.720 --> 00:31:40.860
of a service mesh.

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:43.850
You don't need Istio as long as
you have something like that.

00:31:43.850 --> 00:31:47.850
Istio uses those Envoy proxies
and gives you all that network

00:31:47.850 --> 00:31:49.640
functionality.

00:31:49.640 --> 00:31:51.230
And from an architectural
perspective,

00:31:51.230 --> 00:31:52.550
what it looks like is
something like this.

00:31:52.550 --> 00:31:54.008
And for the past
few minutes, we've

00:31:54.008 --> 00:31:56.420
been talking about the data
plane, how the actual data

00:31:56.420 --> 00:31:57.960
flows back and forth.

00:31:57.960 --> 00:32:00.590
So in this particular
case, every service,

00:32:00.590 --> 00:32:02.150
even if it's outside
of Kubernetes,

00:32:02.150 --> 00:32:05.060
will run with an
out-of-process proxy.

00:32:05.060 --> 00:32:06.980
So in this case, this
is the Envoy proxy

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:09.170
that's running right
below each service.

00:32:09.170 --> 00:32:11.690
And the service will never talk
directly to another service.

00:32:11.690 --> 00:32:14.780
It will go through
its sidecar proxy,

00:32:14.780 --> 00:32:16.880
through the other sidecar
proxy, and then back up.

00:32:16.880 --> 00:32:19.700
And then between the proxies
is where all the networking

00:32:19.700 --> 00:32:21.290
magic and all those
advance feature

00:32:21.290 --> 00:32:23.600
functionality and telemetry,
all of that is happening.

00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:28.210
And services have no clue
of the networking topology.

00:32:28.210 --> 00:32:29.670
Much like everything
else, we need

00:32:29.670 --> 00:32:31.570
a data plane, which we talked
about, and a control plane.

00:32:31.570 --> 00:32:33.570
The control plane consists
of three components--

00:32:33.570 --> 00:32:35.830
the pilot, the mixer,
and the citadel.

00:32:35.830 --> 00:32:38.290
And let's talk about each of
these at a very high level.

00:32:38.290 --> 00:32:40.930
The pilot is the deployment
or the service that actually

00:32:40.930 --> 00:32:42.870
configures those Envoy proxies.

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:46.270
So it talks to every single
Envoy in your service mesh

00:32:46.270 --> 00:32:48.449
and says, this is how
you're constructed.

00:32:48.449 --> 00:32:50.240
This is how you need
to talk to each other.

00:32:50.240 --> 00:32:52.172
It has kind of a north
side and a south side.

00:32:52.172 --> 00:32:53.630
And we talked about
the south side,

00:32:53.630 --> 00:32:55.420
which talks to the Envoy API.

00:32:55.420 --> 00:32:58.180
So it uses those Aggregated
Discovery Services

00:32:58.180 --> 00:33:00.170
to configure the Envoys itself.

00:33:00.170 --> 00:33:03.160
And then on the north side, it
uses other service discovery

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:03.700
services.

00:33:03.700 --> 00:33:05.590
Like it uses the
Kubernetes API to discover

00:33:05.590 --> 00:33:06.980
all the services running.

00:33:06.980 --> 00:33:09.340
It also uses Istio configs,
which are custom resource

00:33:09.340 --> 00:33:12.152
definitions, to actually--
where you can configure yourself

00:33:12.152 --> 00:33:13.610
the route rules,
where you can say,

00:33:13.610 --> 00:33:16.570
I want 50% of the traffic
going to version 1 and 50%

00:33:16.570 --> 00:33:17.500
to version 2.

00:33:17.500 --> 00:33:20.050
It discovers all of that
and then pushes that down

00:33:20.050 --> 00:33:21.660
to the Envoys themselves.

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:25.917
So it's responsible for just
configuring the Envoy proxies.

00:33:25.917 --> 00:33:27.500
Mixer is kind of the
pluggable things.

00:33:27.500 --> 00:33:28.550
It does two things.

00:33:28.550 --> 00:33:30.880
It does policy and
quota enforcement,

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:32.560
and it does
telemetry collection.

00:33:32.560 --> 00:33:34.640
Itself, it's just a collector.

00:33:34.640 --> 00:33:39.320
It's not a logging solution
or a metric solution.

00:33:39.320 --> 00:33:43.739
It's an API that exposes these
metrics out to something.

00:33:43.739 --> 00:33:45.280
So from an observability
perspective,

00:33:45.280 --> 00:33:46.510
those proxies, by
the way, are sitting

00:33:46.510 --> 00:33:48.260
at a very fortunate
position, because they

00:33:48.260 --> 00:33:50.290
get to see every single
request go through them.

00:33:50.290 --> 00:33:53.170
And they can asynchronously
send all these fancy metrics

00:33:53.170 --> 00:33:54.130
to something.

00:33:54.130 --> 00:33:56.440
In this case, that
something is the mixer.

00:33:56.440 --> 00:33:58.780
And then you can plug
in your own solutions

00:33:58.780 --> 00:34:03.556
and your own tooling to
abstract all of these metrics

00:34:03.556 --> 00:34:05.180
and then display them
however you want.

00:34:05.180 --> 00:34:06.910
So for example,
Prometheus has a plug-in

00:34:06.910 --> 00:34:09.250
which comes as a part of
an Istio implementation.

00:34:09.250 --> 00:34:11.417
Prometheus is a
metrics collector

00:34:11.417 --> 00:34:13.750
that connects through a mixer,
collects all the metrics,

00:34:13.750 --> 00:34:15.670
and then you can put some
dashboarding solutions

00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:16.961
like Grafana or something else.

00:34:16.961 --> 00:34:18.610
Stackdriver has a
plug-in where you

00:34:18.610 --> 00:34:21.770
can do all of those
things in a single place.

00:34:21.770 --> 00:34:25.860
It also does policy
and quota enforcement.

00:34:25.860 --> 00:34:28.971
You can have rate limiting
and so on and so forth.

00:34:28.971 --> 00:34:30.429
And then citadel
is the last piece,

00:34:30.429 --> 00:34:32.130
and that does all
the security stuff.

00:34:32.130 --> 00:34:34.330
So it provides
verifiable identity

00:34:34.330 --> 00:34:37.000
in the form of Spiffy IDs.

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:39.190
And then it also provides
a mutual encryption.

00:34:39.190 --> 00:34:42.230
So the citadel is sort of like
a CA or a certificate authority,

00:34:42.230 --> 00:34:44.550
where it's responsible for
distributing certificates,

00:34:44.550 --> 00:34:49.360
certification, and
collecting that.

00:34:49.360 --> 00:34:51.610
So this is where I
just want to show you

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:54.050
kind of an example of
how this all works.

00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:59.790
And let's pray to the demo gods
that everything is working.

00:34:59.790 --> 00:35:01.150
Can we please switch to demo?

00:35:06.020 --> 00:35:08.850
I was told not to
have screen savers.

00:35:08.850 --> 00:35:09.740
OK.

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:11.960
So just to kind of set
the stage on where are,

00:35:11.960 --> 00:35:14.240
we have three clusters,
three Kubernetes clusters.

00:35:14.240 --> 00:35:15.890
They're all running in GCP.

00:35:15.890 --> 00:35:18.200
We have two clusters, one
in central, one in east.

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:21.110
That's going to kind of simulate
our multi-cluster, multicloud

00:35:21.110 --> 00:35:21.735
deployment.

00:35:21.735 --> 00:35:23.360
And then we have a
third cluster that's

00:35:23.360 --> 00:35:24.200
just running our Spinnaker.

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:26.930
So it's just responsible for
just sending applications down

00:35:26.930 --> 00:35:28.210
there.

00:35:28.210 --> 00:35:30.950
I'm just going to show you
an example of a web app.

00:35:30.950 --> 00:35:32.450
It's just a very
simple web app that

00:35:32.450 --> 00:35:35.660
takes the version tag
from the application name

00:35:35.660 --> 00:35:38.300
and puts it back into
the index HTML file.

00:35:38.300 --> 00:35:40.220
So we can just always
see what's running.

00:35:40.220 --> 00:35:43.040
And then we can just
curl that application.

00:35:43.040 --> 00:35:45.722
So if we just do--

00:35:45.722 --> 00:35:47.930
and I'm just going to pick
one of those two clusters,

00:35:47.930 --> 00:35:49.800
but it's essentially
the exact same thing.

00:35:49.800 --> 00:35:52.010
So if we look at the deployments
on the central cluster,

00:35:52.010 --> 00:35:53.000
I have two deployments running.

00:35:53.000 --> 00:35:54.470
So I always have
a canary running,

00:35:54.470 --> 00:35:56.044
and then I have my prod running.

00:35:56.044 --> 00:35:57.710
And the version changes
depending upon--

00:35:57.710 --> 00:35:59.510
the version next
is always canary

00:35:59.510 --> 00:36:01.000
and the version now is prod.

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:03.710
And I always have
four pods of prod

00:36:03.710 --> 00:36:05.820
and one pod of canary running.

00:36:05.820 --> 00:36:11.180
So what I'm going to do right
now is just start a curl loop

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:13.470
so you can just kind
see what's going on.

00:36:13.470 --> 00:36:16.397
So currently in my GKE central
cluster, I have, in my app,

00:36:16.397 --> 00:36:18.230
which is the web front
end, and it's running

00:36:18.230 --> 00:36:20.150
version 103 in production.

00:36:20.150 --> 00:36:22.640
And now I'm a developer,
and I want to upgrade that.

00:36:22.640 --> 00:36:25.190
And I don't really care about
Kubernetes, or Spinnaker,

00:36:25.190 --> 00:36:25.904
or Istio.

00:36:25.904 --> 00:36:27.320
I just want to
upgrade this thing.

00:36:27.320 --> 00:36:29.090
So I'm just going
to simulate that.

00:36:29.090 --> 00:36:30.650
All I'm going to do is
change the version tag

00:36:30.650 --> 00:36:32.275
of the same application
because it will

00:36:32.275 --> 00:36:33.840
update the application itself.

00:36:33.840 --> 00:36:36.470
So I'm going to go
from version 103

00:36:36.470 --> 00:36:40.441
to version 104, just
written right here.

00:36:40.441 --> 00:36:43.140
So if I go here, and I'm just
going to open a second window

00:36:43.140 --> 00:36:45.720
so we can keep that
curl loop going.

00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:47.160
It's a single command.

00:36:47.160 --> 00:36:48.600
It's a container
builder command.

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:50.520
So it takes that
container, and it just

00:36:50.520 --> 00:36:53.520
updates the version tag
of that same container.

00:36:53.520 --> 00:36:57.300
But imagine in your mind
that you've actually

00:36:57.300 --> 00:37:00.215
made some code changes, and then
you've updated the version tag.

00:37:03.010 --> 00:37:04.850
So it's successfully uploaded.

00:37:04.850 --> 00:37:06.850
And let's look at what's
happening in Spinnaker.

00:37:06.850 --> 00:37:07.999
So this is my app.

00:37:07.999 --> 00:37:09.790
That's the same
application that's running.

00:37:09.790 --> 00:37:12.140
I've already done some
iterations to that.

00:37:12.140 --> 00:37:16.060
And you can see that the last
iteration was version 103.

00:37:16.060 --> 00:37:20.680
So I've set the trigger to kick
this pipeline off every time

00:37:20.680 --> 00:37:21.880
the version tag updates.

00:37:21.880 --> 00:37:23.470
So let's look at that pipeline.

00:37:23.470 --> 00:37:25.901
And that pipeline look--
it's a very simple pipeline.

00:37:25.901 --> 00:37:27.400
And you'll notice
there's two roads,

00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:29.525
and they both look very
identical because it's just

00:37:29.525 --> 00:37:31.700
deploying to east and
central at the same time.

00:37:31.700 --> 00:37:33.366
So the first thing
it does is it deploys

00:37:33.366 --> 00:37:37.270
the new version as canary to
both clusters with one pod.

00:37:37.270 --> 00:37:38.560
Then it tests the canary.

00:37:38.560 --> 00:37:41.110
And the way it does that
is it just simply downloads

00:37:41.110 --> 00:37:44.220
a curl utility, BusyBox,
and just curls that service.

00:37:44.220 --> 00:37:45.520
And if it's up, great.

00:37:45.520 --> 00:37:48.460
And then it stops,
and then there's

00:37:48.460 --> 00:37:50.090
a manual judgment
stage where it says,

00:37:50.090 --> 00:37:53.050
do we want to promote
this to production?

00:37:53.050 --> 00:37:55.670
And then when we say yes, it
promotes it to production.

00:37:55.670 --> 00:37:57.670
And the way it gets
triggered is just down here.

00:37:57.670 --> 00:37:59.080
You can see it's a web server.

00:37:59.080 --> 00:38:01.800
And every time this tag
changes, this v tag changes,

00:38:01.800 --> 00:38:02.800
go ahead and trigger it.

00:38:05.272 --> 00:38:07.730
So if we go back, you can see
that the pipeline has already

00:38:07.730 --> 00:38:10.560
triggered for 1.0.4.

00:38:10.560 --> 00:38:12.330
And each of these
boxes is representing

00:38:12.330 --> 00:38:13.210
each of those stages.

00:38:13.210 --> 00:38:15.720
So the first two boxes are
central and east canary.

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:18.450
The next two boxes are testing
the central and east canary.

00:38:18.450 --> 00:38:20.760
And then it stopped
at that fifth box.

00:38:20.760 --> 00:38:22.720
If I just kind of
hover over that,

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:25.309
you can see that it's
asking me to hit Continue.

00:38:25.309 --> 00:38:26.850
So at this point,
what's happening is

00:38:26.850 --> 00:38:30.360
I have four pods of version
1.0.3 deployed as production.

00:38:30.360 --> 00:38:32.520
And then I have one pod
of version 1.0.4 deployed

00:38:32.520 --> 00:38:33.780
as canary.

00:38:33.780 --> 00:38:35.250
So let's look at
our Cloud Shell.

00:38:35.250 --> 00:38:36.480
And what you notice
right off the bat

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:38.340
is that we're still
going to production.

00:38:38.340 --> 00:38:41.590
And that's because I have Istio
installed on this cluster.

00:38:41.590 --> 00:38:45.330
And I've explicitly said
that send 100% of the traffic

00:38:45.330 --> 00:38:46.750
to production.

00:38:46.750 --> 00:38:48.130
So again, I'm a developer.

00:38:48.130 --> 00:38:50.515
I have safely deployed this
thing with a single command.

00:38:50.515 --> 00:38:52.140
I didn't have to
worry about Kubernetes

00:38:52.140 --> 00:38:54.240
deployments or Spinnaker.

00:38:54.240 --> 00:38:56.269
But now I wanted to
send some traffic to it.

00:38:56.269 --> 00:38:58.560
And just for the sake of the
demo, what I'll do is I'll

00:38:58.560 --> 00:39:01.170
send 100% of the
traffic to canary.

00:39:01.170 --> 00:39:02.820
So I'm just going to
copy a route rule.

00:39:05.416 --> 00:39:07.040
I'm going to delete
the route rule that

00:39:07.040 --> 00:39:09.530
says send 100% to
production, and then I'm

00:39:09.530 --> 00:39:13.220
going to implement another
route rule that says send

00:39:13.220 --> 00:39:15.734
100% of the traffic to canary.

00:39:15.734 --> 00:39:17.150
And if we look at
that route rule,

00:39:17.150 --> 00:39:19.024
it's a very simple route
rule that just says,

00:39:19.024 --> 00:39:20.690
look at that label
on that deployment.

00:39:20.690 --> 00:39:22.190
And if that label
says stack canary,

00:39:22.190 --> 00:39:24.680
just go ahead and send
100% of the traffic over.

00:39:24.680 --> 00:39:26.870
And now if we go back,
we should shortly

00:39:26.870 --> 00:39:29.450
see that thing move to 1.0.4.

00:39:29.450 --> 00:39:31.640
And I don't need to worry
about calling a networking

00:39:31.640 --> 00:39:32.431
admin or something.

00:39:32.431 --> 00:39:34.790
And now 100% of the
traffic is going there.

00:39:34.790 --> 00:39:37.010
And then now I can
control these waits.

00:39:37.010 --> 00:39:39.740
And then perhaps I want to
send, you know, 5%, 10%, 20%

00:39:39.740 --> 00:39:43.980
of the traffic without
talking to anybody.

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:45.192
So let's say--

00:39:45.192 --> 00:39:46.900
I mean, just to show
you another example,

00:39:46.900 --> 00:39:48.912
so let's say we
want to send 50/50.

00:39:48.912 --> 00:39:49.620
So I can go back.

00:39:49.620 --> 00:39:55.210
I can delete the 100 canary
rule and just put a 50/50 rule.

00:39:55.210 --> 00:39:57.330
And if we look at what
our route rule looks

00:39:57.330 --> 00:40:01.050
like now, basically by
using the magic of labels

00:40:01.050 --> 00:40:03.150
and saying anything with
the label of stack prod,

00:40:03.150 --> 00:40:04.590
send 50% of the
traffic, anything

00:40:04.590 --> 00:40:07.355
with the label of stack canary,
send 50% of the traffic.

00:40:07.355 --> 00:40:08.730
If we go back to
that now, you'll

00:40:08.730 --> 00:40:12.310
start seeing that traffic
kind of go back and forth.

00:40:12.310 --> 00:40:13.999
So let's say that
we're happy with this.

00:40:13.999 --> 00:40:15.540
The ideal state is,
I want to go back

00:40:15.540 --> 00:40:19.840
to 100% to production traffic.

00:40:19.840 --> 00:40:22.050
So again, I can just
delete that 50/50 rule

00:40:22.050 --> 00:40:25.560
and put that 100%
production traffic back in.

00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:27.090
Now I'm happy with
my deployment.

00:40:27.090 --> 00:40:28.715
I'm going to go ahead
and hit Continue.

00:40:28.715 --> 00:40:34.110
And I'm going to promote
version 1.0.4 to production.

00:40:34.110 --> 00:40:35.870
So those last two
boxes are essentially

00:40:35.870 --> 00:40:39.214
doing a Kubernetes deployment
backed by the same service,

00:40:39.214 --> 00:40:40.880
and they're deploying
four pods of that.

00:40:40.880 --> 00:40:42.713
And while they're
deploying, we can actually

00:40:42.713 --> 00:40:45.600
look at that application and
what it looks like today.

00:40:45.600 --> 00:40:48.390
And so this is in the middle
of it deploying to production.

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:51.250
So we can see that
version 1.0.4,

00:40:51.250 --> 00:40:53.420
there's one pod
deployed as canary.

00:40:53.420 --> 00:40:55.220
And then in production,
I'm starting

00:40:55.220 --> 00:40:58.190
to now create another
deployment for version 1.0.4.

00:40:58.190 --> 00:41:01.310
But in the same screen, I can
see it tearing down the canary

00:41:01.310 --> 00:41:03.290
and putting a new version
up until it's ready.

00:41:03.290 --> 00:41:06.080
These are going to be red until
it waits for every single pod

00:41:06.080 --> 00:41:09.710
to be ready before
it makes them green.

00:41:09.710 --> 00:41:11.980
So let's go back
to our pipeline.

00:41:11.980 --> 00:41:14.590
And let's just make sure
